  <content>
  <section><title>Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
<para>We can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \( y=mx+b \). Now we will do the reverse—we will start with the slope and \( y \)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1 </emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of a line with slope \( −1 \) and \( y \)-intercept \( (0,−3) \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Name the slope. 
  \( m = -1 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Name the \( y \)-intercept. 
  \( b = -3 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \).
  \( y = -1x + -3 \) 
  \( y = -x-3 \) </para>
<para>The same information can be picked up from a graph of a line. </para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2 </emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of the line shown in the graph.</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2aa4ff4aed47d92a6011b39628987c2fa8ee501a"/></media></figure></para>
<para> <emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
  \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
  From \( (0, -4) \) to \( (3,-2) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{2}{3} \). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
  \( y \)-intercept \( (0, -4) \)
  \( b = -4 \) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \). 
  \( y = mx +b \)
  \( y = \frac{2}{3} x - 4 \)</para>

<title>Try It: Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the equation of the line graphed below in slope-intercept form:</para>
    <para><figure><media alt="This figure has a graph of a straight line on the x y-coordinate plane. The x and y-axes run from negative 8 to 8. "><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a2d136d8b31fa1842f0d6e000e386cbe46767603"/></media></figure></para>
  <para>Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:
    Here is how to write the equation of the line in slope-intercept form. </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
    \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
    From \( (0, 1) \) to \( (5, 4) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{3}{5} \). </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
    \( y \)-intercept \( (0, 1) \)
    \( b = 1 \) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b\).
    \( y = mx +b \)
    \( y = \frac{3}{5} x + 1 \)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
